; worldwrite.eval — the world survives being written and read back (W1).
;
; A writer's only real claim is that the thing it wrote is the thing it had. So
; this prints the world, saves it, and prints it again — and WorldTest.py
; compares the two readouts. `saveworld` re-reads the file it just wrote and warns
; if it did not round-trip, which is the same check made where it is cheapest.
;
; It matters because the failure mode is the nastiest kind: a bad write leaves
; the in-memory world correct for the rest of the session, and the damage only
; appears on the next launch.
;
; `saveworld` and not `savemap`: savemap rewrites every LEVEL file too, and a
; level writer regenerates headers — so testing the world writer with it
; silently stripped the authoring notes off eval_arena.
logecho on
reset
world
saveworld
world
